            B.q("ai-m2-1", "ai-acr", "Images older than 30 days that no longer have any tag must be removed every night, with no server of your own. What do you set up?", listOf("A retention policy for untagged manifests: az acr config retention update --status enabled --days 30 --type UntaggedManifests", "A registry webhook on push events whose action deletes untagged manifests older than 30 days", "A scheduled ACR task (az acr task create --schedule \"0 1 * * *\") running acr purge --filter 'recipes-api:^\$' --untagged --ago 30d", "A scheduled ACR task (az acr task create --schedule \"0 1 * * *\") running acr purge --filter 'recipes-api:.*' --ago 30d"), setOf(2), "acr purge is a container command (still in preview) that ACR Tasks can run inside Azure. The tag pattern ^\$ matches no tag names, so no tagged image is touched. --untagged then deletes manifests (stored images) that have no tag, and --ago 30d limits that to ones older than 30 days. A filter such as recipes-api:.* would also delete every tag older than 30 days, including release tags still in use. acr purge --untagged-only --ago 30d does the same job as the ^\$ filter. Creating the task with --schedule, a cron expression (a time pattern such as 0 1 * * * for 01:00 daily), runs it on a timer, and --context /dev/null means it needs no source code. ACR Tasks work in every registry tier.",
