            B.q("ai-m2-16", "ai-cosmos-sdk", "Two devices can save the same plan at nearly the same moment, and the later save must not silently overwrite the earlier one. Which azure-cosmos call shape gives this optimistic concurrency (accept a write only if nobody changed the item since you read it)?", listOf("replace_item(item, body, etag=read_etag, match_condition=MatchConditions.IfNotModified), reloading on 412", "upsert_item(body) on an account set to Strong consistency, so writes are applied in order", "replace_item(item, body, etag=read_etag, match_condition=MatchConditions.IfModified), reloading on 412", "read_item again just before replace_item, and replace only if the _etag still matches"), setOf(0), "Every item has an _etag that changes on each write. Sending the etag you read with IfNotModified makes Cosmos DB reject the write with 412 if someone else wrote in between, so the app can reload and merge.",
                scenario = "Kestrel Travel's Android app lets a family edit one shared trip plan from several phones. Plans are stored in Cosmos DB for NoSQL.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Upsert always writes the new body. Consistency affects what reads see, not whether a write may overwrite another.",
                    2 to "IfModified is the opposite condition: it asks for the operation only when the etag no longer matches, so it does not stop a write over someone else's change.",
                    3 to "Another device can write between that read and your replace, so the check in your own code proves nothing. Only a condition the server checks closes the gap.",
