                    2 to "Database-level throughput is still provisioned throughput, billed every hour whether or not any request arrives.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("ai-m2-18", "ai-cosmos-perf", "Readers may see the feed a little behind, but must never see part of a saved batch, such as a poll without the post it belongs to. Which is the weakest (cheapest) consistency level that guarantees this?", listOf("Eventual", "Consistent prefix", "Bounded staleness", "Strong"), setOf(1), "Consistent prefix means reads may lag, but items written together in one transactional batch become visible together, so a reader sees all of the batch or none of it. Single-item writes on their own get only eventual consistency at this level, which is why the batch matters. Reads use a single replica, at the same price as Session and Eventual.",
                scenario = "Marlow Books runs a reading-club feed in its Android app. When a member posts a poll, the app saves the post and the poll as two items in one transactional batch with the same partition key (the club id). The feed loads a club's items with a single query on that partition key. The Cosmos DB account has one write region and several read regions, and readers never need their own writes back immediately.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "Eventual gives no all-together guarantee, so a reader can see the poll without its post.",
                    2 to "Bounded staleness also gives this guarantee, but it adds a lag limit you do not need, and its reads cost about twice as much.",
                    3 to "Strong gives it too, at the highest read cost and write latency.",
                ),
