            B.q("ai-m2-36", "ai-servicebus", "Recipe requests older than 10 minutes are useless and must not be processed, but support wants to count the expired ones instead of losing them. What do you configure?", listOf("A 10 minute time to live set on each message, and nothing else", "A 10 minute time to live plus max_delivery_count set to 1 on the queue", "A 10 minute time to live plus dead-lettering on filter evaluation errors", "A 10 minute time to live plus dead-lettering on message expiration"), setOf(3), "Time to live (TTL) makes a message expire. By default an expired message is simply dropped; with dead-lettering on message expiration turned on (dead_lettering_on_message_expiration), it moves to the dead-letter queue with the reason TTLExpiredException, where it can be counted.",
                scenario = "Pantry Pal's Android app asks an AI back end for recipe suggestions through a Service Bus queue. If a request waits more than 10 minutes, the user has already left the screen.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "TTL alone expires the message, but with expiry dead-lettering off the expired messages are dropped, so there is nothing to count.",
                    1 to "Max delivery count limits failed processing attempts. With expiry dead-lettering off, expired messages are still dropped, so there is nothing to count.",
                    2 to "Dead-lettering on filter evaluation errors is a topic subscription setting for messages whose filter rule fails. A queue has no filters, and it does not catch expired messages.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("ai-m2-37", "ai-servicebus", "A message arrives before the data it depends on exists. The worker wants to set this message aside, keep it away from other receivers, and fetch it again later by its number. Which calls?", listOf("abandon_message, then wait for it to be delivered again", "defer_message, then receive_deferred_messages with its sequence number", "dead_letter_message, then read it back from the dead-letter queue", "renew_message_lock repeatedly until the data it needs has arrived"), setOf(1), "Deferring a message leaves it in the queue but hides it from normal receives. Only a receiver that asks for it by its sequence number (a unique number Service Bus gives every message) can get it back.",
                scenario = "FitPulse, a workout tracking app, processes WorkoutFinished messages, but sometimes the matching WorkoutStarted message has not been processed yet.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "Abandon puts the message straight back on the queue and adds to its delivery count, so it can spin until it is dead-lettered.",
                    2 to "The dead-letter queue is for messages that cannot be processed. Mixing valid messages in hides real failures and needs a resubmit step.",
                    3 to "Renewing the lock keeps the message tied to this worker and receiver for as long as the wait lasts. It is not set aside, and it cannot be fetched again later by its number.",
