                ),
            ),
            B.q("ai-m2-55", "ai-opentelemetry", "Inside a custom span, a call to the speech service raises an exception that the code catches and handles. The span must still show as failed, with the error details. What do you add in the except block? Select two.", listOf("span.record_exception(e)", "span.set_attribute(\"failed\", \"maybe\")", "span.set_status(Status(StatusCode.ERROR))", "A second call to trace.get_tracer(__name__)"), setOf(0, 2), "record_exception attaches the exception (type, message and stack trace) to the span as an event, and set_status with StatusCode.ERROR marks the span as failed, so it counts as a failure in Application Insights. An exception that escapes the with block is recorded for you; one you catch yourself is not.",
                scenario = "Fieldnote's Android app uploads voice memos to a Python API, which sends them to a speech service and retries in code when the call fails.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "A free-form attribute is only a label. It does not change the span's status, so the span still counts as a success.",
                    3 to "Getting the tracer again creates nothing and records nothing about the error.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("ai-m2-56", "ai-opentelemetry", "Calls from the API to Azure Managed Redis through redis-py never appear as dependencies, although its HTTP calls do. What do you add?", listOf("A second call to configure_azure_monitor() after the Redis client is created", "OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=redis on the API container, so Redis gets its own node", "The opentelemetry-instrumentation-requests package, with RequestsInstrumentor().instrument()", "The opentelemetry-instrumentation-redis package, with RedisInstrumentor().instrument()"), setOf(3), "The Azure Monitor distro bundles a fixed set of instrumentations (web frameworks, requests, psycopg2, the Azure SDK and a few more). A library outside that set needs its own OpenTelemetry instrumentation package. Once it is instrumented, every Redis command becomes a dependency span inside the request's trace.",
                scenario = "Kitchenly's Python API caches recipe search results in Azure Managed Redis and sends telemetry to Application Insights.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "Setting up the pipeline twice does not add instrumentation for a library it does not cover.",
                    1 to "That would rename the API itself on the application map. It does not capture Redis calls.",
                    2 to "The requests instrumentation records HTTP calls made with the requests library, which already appear. redis-py speaks the Redis protocol over TCP, not HTTP.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("ai-m2-57", "ai-opentelemetry", "Container Apps health probes call /health every few seconds, and those requests make up most of the telemetry bill. How do you stop tracing them without losing other requests?", listOf("Lower the trace sampling rate to 1 percent for all requests", "Set OTEL_PYTHON_EXCLUDED_URLS to health on the API container", "Raise the probes' interval so they call /health far less often", "Delete the probe rows from the requests table every night"), setOf(1), "The OpenTelemetry Python web instrumentations read a list of excluded URLs (OTEL_PYTHON_EXCLUDED_URLS, or a framework-specific one such as OTEL_PYTHON_FASTAPI_EXCLUDED_URLS) and skip matching requests. The probes keep working; they simply stop producing telemetry.",
                scenario = "Parcelly's tracking API runs on Azure Container Apps with liveness and readiness probes, and sends traces to Application Insights.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "Sampling cuts every kind of trace alike, so real user requests are lost along with the probes.",
                    2 to "Fewer probe calls are still traced, so the probes still produce telemetry, and a long interval also makes the platform slower to notice an unhealthy replica.",
                    3 to "You pay to ingest data when it arrives. Deleting it later does not undo that cost.",
